WEBVTT

1
00:00.550 --> 00:10.420
This is how Crosswalk digital twins work: predictive, AI driven outcomes built on real behavior, tested and vetted by some of the biggest brands in the world.

2
00:10.420 --> 00:14.730
And why no other digital twin or synthetic data company can say that.

3
00:16.380 --> 00:18.079
It starts with real people.

4
00:18.079 --> 00:23.614
Crosswalk collects the digital lives of thirty million opted-in people around the world.

5
00:23.614 --> 00:29.842
Inside that is a ten million person US panel, census matched, so it looks exactly like the country.

6
00:29.842 --> 00:32.170
Every age, every income, every state.

7
00:33.820 --> 00:39.194
Every person in that panel gets a digital twin: a working copy of how they behave.

8
00:39.194 --> 00:43.715
What they watch, search, buy, pay for and come back to, and how fast.

9
00:43.715 --> 00:44.633
Not a summary.

10
00:44.633 --> 00:46.730
The whole sequence, with timing.

11
00:48.380 --> 00:52.277
Each twin's personality comes from history, not a questionnaire.

12
00:52.277 --> 00:56.783
Five years of observed behavior, back to January first, twenty twenty-one.

13
00:56.783 --> 01:01.410
So the twin talks like its person, worries like them, and decides like them.

14
01:03.060 --> 01:04.653
Then the twins run forward.

15
01:04.653 --> 01:09.610
They generate synthetic data: what each person will do next, not only what they did.

16
01:11.260 --> 01:18.043
When you build a twin group, it pulls from a single source of truth: the behavior of those ten million Americans.

17
01:18.043 --> 01:23.746
Each seat stands for a slice of the country, so the group scales up to the whole US population.

18
01:23.746 --> 01:26.988
Our standard deviation is plus or minus three percent.

19
01:26.988 --> 01:30.770
In short, you can talk to all of America, and trust the answer.

20
01:32.420 --> 01:37.829
Ask the group questions, just like you would ask real people if you were in a room with them.

21
01:37.829 --> 01:43.296
Their responses come back in seconds, in percentages and in people, with the voices behind it.

22
01:43.296 --> 01:46.960
Then change the price, the cast or the platform, and ask again.

23
01:48.610 --> 01:49.436
Does it work?

24
01:49.436 --> 01:55.341
On election night twenty twenty-six, the twins called Texas and Ohio before the polls closed.

25
01:55.341 --> 02:00.485
In Texas, the room read Talarico fifty-four point six to forty-three point three.

26
02:00.485 --> 02:03.533
Texas landed at about fifty-five to forty-three.

27
02:03.533 --> 02:07.153
In Ohio, the room had Acton from the first ask, days out.

28
02:07.153 --> 02:08.296
She won the state.

29
02:08.296 --> 02:15.599
Now move from the big races to a small House seat in Nevada: the fourth district, fewer than half a million voters.

30
02:15.599 --> 02:20.298
The room said Horsford by twenty-three, and the district landed there too.

31
02:20.298 --> 02:22.330
The same accuracy at every size.

32
02:23.980 --> 02:28.432
This is a pilot test, with the NCIS: New York pilot in front of the group.

33
02:28.432 --> 02:38.840
The dial moves second by second as the group watches, every reaction lands on the clock, and the chat on the side lets you ask the group why, while the cut is still playing.

34
02:40.490 --> 02:45.196
Before it aired, we said week one would draw ten point zero four million viewers.

35
02:45.196 --> 02:49.204
It drew ten point two five million, the fall's number one new series.

36
02:49.204 --> 02:52.864
We said eighty-three point nine percent would finish the pilot.

37
02:52.864 --> 02:56.873
In the end, eighty-one point six percent finished it within the week.

38
02:56.873 --> 03:00.126
We said eighty-five point seven percent would come back.

39
03:00.126 --> 03:03.960
In fact, eighty-six point eight percent came back for episode two.

40
03:05.610 --> 03:13.462
The room also found the biggest draw for new franchise viewers: Jennifer Beals, a supporting role with ninety seconds on screen.

41
03:13.462 --> 03:15.547
After air, the panel confirmed it.

42
03:15.547 --> 03:24.994
She brought three in ten viewers, and brought the most directly attributable new Paramount Plus subscriptions, with their first view being NCIS: New York.

43
03:24.994 --> 03:33.950
This data was used in real time: they put her on The Tonight Show and featured her prominently on social media, to bring her audience to the show.

44
03:35.600 --> 03:38.013
The same twins work before any decision.

45
03:38.013 --> 03:40.607
Test a pilot, then keep testing the series.

46
03:40.607 --> 03:43.745
A B test an ad before the buy, down to the zip code.

47
03:43.745 --> 03:45.977
Put two prices in front of the group.

48
03:45.977 --> 03:48.933
Get the odds on an idea before you greenlight it.

49
03:48.933 --> 03:52.010
Run a media plan forward before the money goes out.

50
03:52.010 --> 03:55.690
Or keep the room seated all season and watch every move land.

51
03:57.340 --> 03:58.702
Here is the difference.

52
03:58.702 --> 04:05.392
Other digital twins are built on what people say: surveys, interviews, or a language model guessing at a persona.

53
04:05.392 --> 04:07.879
And they are checked against more surveys.

54
04:07.879 --> 04:13.089
Crosswalk twins are built on what ten million real people do, every day, for five years.

55
04:13.089 --> 04:18.240
And they are checked against real outcomes: elections called, ratings, accounts opened.

56
04:19.890 --> 04:21.859
Predictive, AI driven outcomes.

57
04:21.859 --> 04:23.765
Built on real behavioral data.

58
04:23.765 --> 04:27.640
Tested and vetted by some of the biggest brands in the world.

59
04:29.290 --> 04:32.815
Talk to all of America before you spend a dollar.

60
04:32.815 --> 04:34.830
Sign up at crosswalknyc.com.
